# __MODULE_SLUG__

DEVELOPMENT - TEST DATA ONLY. This is a framework, not a completed application.

## Standalone

1. Install Node 22+, run `npm install`, and commit the generated package-lock.json.
   Switch CI/Docker installs to `npm ci` once that lockfile is committed.
2. Configure the names in `.env.example` in your environment; never commit values.
   For local development load them with your shell or Node's `--env-file` option.
3. Use a separate development PostgreSQL database, never the Global Control one.
4. Configure a dedicated Google OAuth client and register
   `https://YOUR-SERVICE/auth/google/callback` as an authorised redirect URI.
5. Set `MODULE_ALLOWED_EMAILS` to the explicit school tester allowlist. School
   domain membership alone never grants access. No local bypass is provided.
6. Run `npm test`, then `npm start` (runs versioned migrations before starting).
   `npm run db:migrate` can also be run separately. `/health` checks the database, not user access.
   The service deliberately returns 503 for application pages until Google
   configuration is present. An empty tester allowlist denies all sign-ins.

The standalone service uses database-backed Google sessions. No Global Control
cookies, secrets, production database credentials or trusted identity headers are
used. Development credentials and emails must be separate from production.

## Embedded integration contract

The intended mount is `__MOUNT_PATH__`. Exported `createModuleRouter` must be
mounted behind Global Control authentication; it reads server-supplied `req.user`.
Use `req.baseUrl` and relative links for nested routes. The standalone Google
adapter is not mounted when embedded. The host owns Google identity, avatar,
Settings, sign-out and primary navigation.

Add numbered SQL files under `db/migrations`; successful migration names are
recorded in `module_migrations`. Migrations run transactionally on one connection
with a transaction-level advisory lock. The initial migration is a no-data baseline.

Before business data is added, define owner/sharing rules, reviewed migrations,
backups, CSRF protection for mutations, safe external effects, audit logging and
an integration test. Do not treat a Teacher role as authority over all records.
Supply a narrowly scoped module database dependency when adding data access.

## Release checklist

- Development/staging/production have separate credentials and data.
- No real emails, calendar writes or confidential records in development.
- User access, tenant/record ownership and denied access are tested.
- Migrations, restore, rollback, mobile, accessibility and any export/print
  requirements are tested in Global Control staging before release.
- Nothing automatically promotes or embeds this repository into production.
